            "title": "HDGfx Infector",
            "content": "Overview . HDGfx Infector is a Windows x86 file infector and USB worm built in mid-2026. As of 2026-11-17 there appears to be no public reporting on it despite its widespread propagation via sandboxes, specificaly the VirusTotal &quot;sandbox centipede&quot;. . The malware is a small MinGW-w64 (GCC 8.1.0) DLL. Its export directory names it System.dll, and its export names describe what it does: InfectFile, DisinfectFile, MetamorphicStub, GeneratePolymorphicAutorunInf and so on. It infects 32-bit EXEs in place: . A junk-filled entry stub overwrites the host entry point. | A layer decryptor and an encrypted loader are added to the end of the host&#39;s last section. | The loader decrypts the UPX-packed core DLL with RC4, maps it in memory, puts back the original entry bytes and returns to the host. | . Every infection gets new keys and newly generated junk code. Once running, the core: . infects EXEs on every fixed and removable drive; | copies itself to each drive root as a hidden .exe or .pif with a locked, randomised autorun.inf; | polls a No-IP dynamic-DNS host on TCP 8000 to download and run an update. | . Key points . Active development. There are two builds about six weeks apart (version banners 15/07/2026 and 23/08/2026). The second adds a second update host and a new payload cipher. | Infects trusted software. Infected files include Adobe, Java, Firefox and Google Update components. Infection removes their Authenticode signatures. | Targets AV installers. It breaks files whose names contain bitdef or kasp. | Noisy. Each infected program plays a sequence of beeps when it starts. | Still spreading. The earliest sample we have was first seen on 2026-10-22. New infected files were still appearing on 2026-11-17. | . Name . &quot;HDGfx Infector&quot; is our working name. The malware has no family name, PDB path, copyright or version resource. The name comes from the only product-like string in the code, Intel(R) HD Graphics Manager.exe. This is the file name the worm asks its update server for. . The identifiers the malware does carry: . Identifier Value . DLL export name | System.dll | . Version banner v1 | ======================= VERSION 15/07/2026 ====================== | . Version banner v2 | ======================= VERSION 23/08/2026 ====================== | . Mutex (v1 only) | @0826 | . Compiler | GCC: (i686-posix-dwarf-rev0, Built by MinGW-W64 project) 8.1.0 | . Timeline . Build PE timestamp Banner date Infection marker (dword) . v1 | 2026-07-24 05:48:59 | 15/07/2026 | 0x20260715 | . v2 | 2026-08-26 20:42:41 | 23/08/2026 | 0x20260823 | . The infection marker is the banner date as BCD. | In both builds the banner date is a few days before the PE timestamp. | The author controls all of these values, so they are consistent but not proof of the build dates. | . v1 treats any marker at or below 0x20260714 as an old version and re-infects the file. That suggests builds older than 15 July 2026 exist, but we have not found one. . Samples . Every infected host and both memory images below decrypt to the same v1 core, ec55c4ba2ab0b530c4d613e9c0214611c734fc0d17e2fabec68ade33b980075c. . Core DLL . SHA256 Description . ec55c4ba2ab0b530c4d613e9c0214611c734fc0d17e2fabec68ade33b980075c | v1 core, UPX 4.24 packed (the encrypted payload in every host) | . 80b49269fa01f64e34960f99922f93c53e0370d94c3158dac450281defb4d0bf | v1 core, unpacked | . fbcbcd9c743695119f822da7d57e7f4605c93513b8cd70ed598d2aa942363af2 | v1 core, sandbox memory dump | . 967c2c5bd65e8dc3fd46007e9ef19b5c117a5335d83080d0e9ffe61cf0d94041 | v1 core, sandbox memory dump | . 0678e636166df303548c8c2f4d75611304f7f01d5881ac6e4baad16dae0aa566 | v2 core, memory dump (relocated to base 0x036A0000) | . Infected hosts . The 25 hosts come from 16 different programs. . SHA256 Host program . a0c42a23fbcd3cd8c21db68517e18b5c229e9a2d95af62a5a57407ff92e48141 | Adobe Systems 1.824.35.0289 | . b448a0be6e7c3c25905bf6c97ecda197d3ef8f4f362a8920c8974c3c32cfe354 | Adobe Systems 1.824.35.0289 | . 44a58d2e7d0f114939e067b1645fcc171ce60ffaa8ee918b7a35f2acd44a4945 | Adobe Systems 1.824.35.0289 | . 5d90689c226a121eeca04ab5676430ed608a6d0f0c1339fb1684497346de9f82 | Adobe Systems 1.824.35.0289 | . 7b232565f9697568eb4cc89dc49bf956ba2ee401a820dce93f28b6af704e8fd6 | AdobeARM.exe 1.824.35.0289 | . 3cd13ce7ff46c49466c5484c0d7c08be4d97944288d356f2e1472dfa8c606e28 | AdobeARM.exe 1.824.35.0289 | . 8015cea2882398190b58093a0715c5b21bd53510e7186c3db7f36e78cdacc349 | Adobe Inc. 1.824.460.1032 | . 609372a70bd5b35befcf8e99a83355a049618f8cd9e6d53102a17811abfaa123 | Adobe Inc. 1.824.460.1032 | . cd551a44e7673c9a4a2007a7574ac1b1be7e439a3705461d5de07af70f90310c | Adobe Setup.exe 20.6.20034.366983 | . 59163fa30be47fbcce61171774d81f0283611ef51d2100561f501b3180dd8802 | Adobe Setup.exe 20.6.20034.366983 | . 727966a9ec7f46a0227dcaf39be22193f24f2650e48541013bc90e3456286f90 | Adobe Setup.exe 20.6.20034.366983 | . 249ea8bf7b6a76694253970e9312ee88d3e2144fe56479cfcad19966f827e834 | Adobe Setup.exe 18.9.20044.251705 | . 8dd3e8a7ce2a7e4d5566603a67833a4ff97c8b1cd84010edbf63765d9681762b | Adobe Setup.exe 18.9.20044.251705 | . 51c263c86d5933248e4ba7b186eb12b5afa87c1a26be7dffc9ff1516efab9d0f | AdobeCollabSync.exe 18.11.20055.290043 | . 76f52cf20a71cb883c8c4048c14e3a88b6a5590a99a4f59c9d162f45e58c9471 | AcroBroker.exe 20.6.20034.366983 | . 115e3d313832e8b316052d65848a8d236a17444c3d1c87818e3be0c4e96745fd | no version info (7.2 MB) | . 9ce158f5767ca6ed78677a1a3a64b481c9e3a5a3e763b777290e65290c275e5e | Oracle javacpl.exe 11.241.2.07 | . 93f1d015de77d727bb33081fef5ef0fc4098e256c554950bc539fd77cd73e349 | Oracle javaw.exe 8.0.2410.7 | . fdc0a130b156a7fdbae7d55896f245cec9db4d30ef8174a6531efa6f9b272705 | Oracle jp2launcher.exe 11.381.2.09 | . d051dfb9a5e492bf1999b193656a76091cc9946403c42dd2b941b30f66246987 | Mozilla maintenanceservice.exe 61.0.1 | . 418871b8b598100a5f5e399e01a89422ca0a2a5b770fbc18cc58e352bb07a31c | Mozilla crashreporter.exe 61.0.1 | . 949f2bae70c44b36d0583d4f2c012a97aa3e2ba5e0c85be6302fcbbf64aab429 | Mozilla plugin-container.exe 61.0.1 | . 795d8d2e4360bc1e39c67b02dae1601e4847169fc2f05cef261de1c0ccc64e61 | GoogleUpdateSetup.exe 1.3.36.152 | . 4685a9c8f49f7d176caa00559f014aeb8b6cf6660d3c279f58dfadaea31700e7 | GoogleUpdateSetup.exe 1.3.36.152 | . 649b448d5d617ecc4f55f999dede56386503249f4eabc64754ef8e0a013043e8 | nerds.de loopBeAudio.exe 1.2.0.0 | . Memory images of infected processes . These two files are process-memory images of infected programs, probably dumped by a sandbox. The layer is already decrypted and the original entry bytes are restored. . SHA256 Host program . 2192360d42246a9ed475215fe7c6afed67d15ac80782bd52e44b8cdc74de77c0 | eFigureOut MouseLocator.EXE 1,0,0,1 | . c88c2d9c0d8059e60b1e9eb374b8d8c58c8077b10cb264b13ebb3ff0f78f0d66 | no version info | . Analysis . The function names below are the malware&#39;s own export names. Addresses are RVAs in the v1 core (ImageBase 0x62400000) unless marked otherwise. The example host is a0c42a23 (ImageBase 0x400000). . Infection Chain . An infected program runs in this order: . The entry stub at the original entry point decodes a small transfer block and jumps to the layer decryptor. | The layer decryptor in the last section decrypts the layer and jumps to the loader. | The loader decrypts and maps the core DLL, then calls its DllMain. | DllMain starts the worm threads and copies the original entry bytes back. | The loader jumps to the original entry point and the host runs normally. | entry stub (host EP, .text, RWX) last section (.reloc/.rsrc, RWX, enlarged) +-+ ++ | 4-7 junk blocks | push/ret | original section data | 100-200 junk blocks | decryptor+key | | XOR-0x5F decoder | -&gt; | ... | -&gt; decrypt layer, jmp | mult | count | | transfer block | +-++ +-+ | ENCRYPTED LAYER: loader junk (100-200 blocks) | loader | | (0x349 B) | API names | RC4(core DLL, UPX) 0x8C00 B | stolen bytes | | flag | METADATA BOX 0x28 B (rc4 key ... marker 20260715) | ++ . The malware&#39;s log strings call these stages S0 (entry stub), S0Spec (transfer block), S1 (layer decryptor) and S2 (loader). . Junk Code . Every stage is padded with generated junk blocks (GenerateJunkBlock, 0x2D31). Each block: . starts with push 0FFF4F7xxh / pop r32 (bytes 68 xx F7 F4 FF 5x), where xx is random; | does some register arithmetic (xchg, add [esp], rol/ror, imul, movsx ...); | ends with an opaque branch that is always taken, a few dead bytes, and xor r,r. | . The templates for each stage also get small instruction substitutions. For example test ecx,ecx becomes or ecx,ecx, and add eax,13h becomes sub eax,-13h. Infected hosts have 4-7 junk blocks in the entry stub and 100-200 in each of the layer decryptor and the loader. . Entry Point Stub . InfectFile overwrites the bytes at the original entry point (OEP) with the entry stub and saves the original bytes inside the encrypted layer. The stub has three parts: . 4-7 junk blocks. | A XOR decoder (xor byte [esi],5Fh over N bytes, then push edi / ret). The key is always 0x5F in v1. | The encoded transfer block. It computes the address of the layer decryptor and returns into it. | The decoder in host a0c42a23: . 00415d00 e8e6ffffff call 0x415ceb ; pushes 0x415d05 (encoded transfer block) 00415ceb 5e pop esi 00415cec 89f7 mov edi, esi 00415cee b93c000000 mov ecx, 0x3c ; template has 0DEADBEEFh here 00415cf3 09c9 or ecx, ecx 00415cf5 7407 je 0x415cfe 00415cf7 80365f xor byte ptr [esi], 0x5f 00415cfa 46 inc esi 00415cfb 49 dec ecx 00415cfc ebf5 jmp 0x415cf3 00415cfe 57 push edi 00415cff c3 ret ; -&gt; decoded transfer block . The decoded transfer block adds a stored delta to its own address and returns to the layer decryptor: . 00415d05 e800000000 call 0x415d0a 00415d0a 5b pop ebx ; ebx = 0x415d0a 00415d0b 0f6ec3 movd mm0, ebx ; MMX round trip 00415d0e 0f7ec7 movd edi, mm0 00415d11 b8f6550500 mov eax, 0x555f6 ; delta ... ; junk 00415d3a 01c7 add edi, eax ; edi = 0x46b300 = layer decryptor entry 00415d3c 57 push edi 00415d40 c3 ret . In all 25 hosts the size of the entry stub matches the saved-bytes size in the metadata box, and the transfer block&#39;s target matches the layer decryptor entry in the box. . Layer Decryptor . The layer decryptor starts with 100-200 junk blocks. Its core is a short decryptor followed by three dwords: the key, the multiplier and the byte count. . 0046cc86 e800000000 call 0x46cc8b 0046cc8b 5d pop ebp 0046cc8c 8d7d3c lea edi, [ebp + 0x3c] 0046cc8f 8b0f mov ecx, [edi] ; count (0xa335) 0046cc91 8b47f8 mov eax, [edi - 8] ; key (0x097b1a00) 0046cc94 8b57fc mov edx, [edi - 4] ; mult (27) 0046cc97 8d7704 lea esi, [edi + 4] ; layer start 0046cc9a 89f5 mov ebp, esi 0046cc9c 85c9 test ecx, ecx 0046cc9e 741d je 0x46ccbd 0046cca0 0fafc2 imul eax, edx ; a = a * mult 0046cca3 83e8ed sub eax, -0x13 ; a = a + 0x13 0046cca6 89c3 mov ebx, eax 0046cca8 c1e303 shl ebx, 3 0046ccab c1e81d shr eax, 0x1d 0046ccae 09d8 or eax, ebx ; a = rol(a, 3) 0046ccb0 88c3 mov bl, al 0046ccb2 301e xor byte ptr [esi], bl ; decrypt one byte 0046ccb4 0fb61e movzx ebx, byte ptr [esi] 0046ccb7 01d8 add eax, ebx ; feed back the plaintext byte 0046ccb9 46 inc esi 0046ccba 49 dec ecx 0046ccbb ebdf jmp 0x46cc9c 0046ccbd ffe5 jmp ebp ; -&gt; loader . The cipher in short: . a = key for each byte b: a = rol32(a * mult + 0x13, 3) b ^= a &amp; 0xFF a += b # plaintext byte . InfectFile picks a new key and multiplier for each infection. The key is rand() &lt;&lt; 16 ^ rand(). The multiplier is (rand() % 126) | 1, an odd value from 1 to 125. rand is a standard LCG (x * 0x41C64E6D + 0x3039) seeded with GetTickCount. . Loader . The loader is 0x349 bytes of position-independent code. It: . Saves all registers (pushad / pushfd). | Reads the host base from the PEB and finds kernel32 as the third entry in the module initialisation-order list. | Finds GetProcAddress by comparing the first 8 characters of each export name, then resolves VirtualAlloc, Beep and LoadLibraryA. | Plays beeps: 440 Hz, 660 Hz and 880 Hz. | Finds the metadata box at the end of the last section. | Copies the payload into new RWX memory and decrypts it with RC4 (8-byte key from the box). | Checks for MZ, maps the sections, applies relocations and resolves imports. | Beeps again (880 Hz, 1200 Hz) and calls the DLL entry point with DLL_PROCESS_ATTACH. | Restores registers and jumps to the original entry point. | The loader does not restore the stolen entry bytes. The core does that in ExecuteTrampoline (0xCEA6), which DllMain calls before it returns: . it finds the box at the end of the host&#39;s last section; | it checks that the stolen size is between 1 and 0x258; | it copies the saved bytes back to the OEP with VirtualProtect; | it beeps once more at 1500 Hz. | . In host a0c42a23 the saved bytes disassemble to the original MSVC start-up code (call __security_init_cookie / jmp __tmainCRTStartup). . Metadata Box . The last 0x28 bytes of the decrypted layer hold the infection metadata. Values are from host a0c42a23. . Offset Field Example . +0x00 | RC4 key for the core (8 bytes, new per infection) | 291c58e31f3b7a7d | . +0x08 | Size of the saved entry bytes (= entry stub size, max 0x258) | 0x184 | . +0x0C | OEP RVA | 0x15BBD | . +0x10 | Layer decryptor entry RVA | 0x6B300 | . +0x14 | Payload offset inside the layer | 0x1575 | . +0x18 | Layer RVA (= start of the loader) | 0x6CCCB | . +0x1C | Payload size (always 0x8C00) | 0x8C00 | . +0x20 | Payload RVA | 0x6E240 | . +0x24 | Version marker | 0x20260715 | . Just before the box: . end - 0x30 is a flag. It is 0 in infected files and 0x5354 in the USB copy. | end - 0x2C holds a value we could not identify. | The saved entry bytes sit before that, aligned to 16 bytes. | . When the worm infects the next file it reads its own RC4 key from this box, decrypts its own core, and re-encrypts it with a new key. . What Gets Infected . InfectFile (0x54FE) accepts a file only if: . it is a PE32 i386 EXE between 0x200 bytes and 40 MB; | it is not .NET (the COM descriptor directory must be empty); | the entry section is code or executable; | it is not already infected with the current or a newer marker. | . Overlay handling: . An Authenticode signature overlay is accepted (&quot;Overlay is Digital Signature. Safe to infect.&quot;). It is kept as overlay data, but the Security directory is zeroed, so Windows no longer sees a signature. | An overlay larger than file size / 5, or larger than 512,000 bytes, is skipped (&quot;Massive Overlay&quot;). | An overlay that starts with a RAR, ZIP or 7z signature is skipped (&quot;SFX Archive Signature&quot;). | Any other overlay is backed up and restored. | . InfectFile has three patch modes: . DIRECT_PATCH writes the entry stub at the OEP. All 25 hosts use it. | STUB_PATCH is used when the OEP is in the last section. | An entry-point-obscuring mode searches for three byte patterns and writes an E8 call. We have not seen a sample that uses it. | . Header changes made to every infected file: . the entry section and the last section are set to RWX; | the last section grows to hold the layer decryptor and the layer; | DYNAMIC_BASE and NX_COMPAT are cleared, and RELOCS_STRIPPED is set; | the checksum and the Security directory are zeroed. | . Our reading of why: clearing DYNAMIC_BASE keeps the host at its preferred base, so no relocation touches the patched entry code. Clearing NX_COMPAT lets the RWX code run. . DisinfectFile (0x511C) reverses an infection. It finds the layer decryptor, decrypts the layer, restores the entry bytes and truncates the file. The worm uses it to upgrade files that carry an older marker. . Startup . On DLL_PROCESS_ATTACH, DllMain (0xD1ED): . turns off error dialogs and beeps at 750 Hz; | creates a private heap and an infection mutex; | if it can take the @0826 mutex, starts five threads: DownloadAndUpdate, CleanAllUsersTemp, ScanFile, CreateAutoRun and RegScanThread; | otherwise starts MonitorMutex, which retries every 5 seconds and starts the threads when the mutex is free; | calls ExecuteTrampoline and returns, so the host program runs normally while the threads work. | File Scanning . ScanFile (0xB491) runs in two phases: . Phase 1 starts one worker per removable or fixed drive from C: to Z: and builds a list of EXEs. | Phase 2 infects the files in the list, with a 2-second pause after each one. | . A full cycle ends with a sleep of 500 seconds, or 5 seconds when nothing was found. . The scanner skips these folders: $RECYCLE.BIN, System Volume Information, Sandbox, Program Data, Application Data, Roaming, Local Settings, Documents and Settings, Microsoft Shared, Documents, Microsoft, Locallow, Temp, Windows. It also skips files named sality.exe and bdagent.exe. . The code logs &quot;SKIPPING Program Files path&quot;, but the check only tests whether the path starts with c: aa. This looks like a leftover test path. In practice Program Files is scanned, which fits the infected Adobe, Java, Firefox and Google Update files. . RegScanThread (0x74B8) reads the values under HKCU Software Microsoft Windows CurrentVersion Run and infects each EXE they point to. Programs that start with Windows are infected first. This is the worm&#39;s only persistence. It never writes to the registry. . USB and Drive Spreading . Every 5 seconds CreateAutoRun (0xCBF2) does the following for every removable or fixed drive: . Builds a new encrypted copy of the core with a new RC4 key. | Picks a file name. It re-uses the program named in an existing autorun.inf, or generates 4-6 random lowercase letters with .exe or .pif. | Writes a standalone EXE to the drive root and sets it read-only, hidden and system. | Writes a randomised autorun.inf and holds it open with an exclusive lock so it cannot be deleted. | The standalone EXE is built from scratch: one .text section holding the entry stub, the layer decryptor and the encrypted layer, with the box flag set to 0x5354. When it runs, it opens Explorer on the drive root so the user sees the drive contents. It then keeps running for about 10 minutes while the threads infect files, and exits. . The autorun.inf contains [AutoRun] and six entries in random order: open=, shellexecute=, shell open default=1, shell open command=, shell explore command= and shell autoplay command=. The generator randomises letter case, spacing around =, comment lines and blank lines, so no two files are the same. . Windows 7 and later ignore autorun.inf on USB flash drives. XP and Vista got the same change in 2011 (KB971029). On current systems the USB copy only runs if someone opens it. Infected EXEs on removable drives still spread the worm without AutoRun. . Update Channel . DownloadAndUpdate (0x10C2) connects to the update host on TCP port 8000. The protocol is plain TCP with NUL-terminated ASCII words: . Client sends DOWNLOAD. | Client waits 50 ms and sends Intel(R) HD Graphics Manager.exe. | Server sends a 4-byte little-endian length (1 byte to 10 MiB). | Client sends READY. | Server sends the file in chunks of up to 4,096 bytes. | The client writes the file to %TEMP% with 6 random letters and .exe, starts it with CreateProcessW, and the thread ends. If the download is incomplete, it deletes the file and tries again after 5 seconds. Socket timeouts are 30 seconds. There is no HTTP, no encryption and no check of the server or the file. . On 2026-11-17 updatewin[.]ddns[.]net resolved to 192.168.1[.]100, a private LAN address, with a TTL of 60. updatewinold[.]ddns[.]net had no A record. We did not connect to either host, so we do not know what the server sends. A private address fits an author testing on a home or lab network. The name can be pointed at a public server at any time. . Other Behaviour . AV installer sabotage. If the file name contains bitdef or kasp, the patch writes eight RET (0xC3) bytes instead of a call, so the program returns at once. This is from the decompiled code only; we have no sample that shows it. | Temp cleaning. Every 10 seconds CleanAllUsersTemp deletes *.exe and *.rar from every user&#39;s Temp folder. The code does not show why. | Beeps. The loader, DllMain and ExecuteTrampoline beep at 440, 660, 880, 880, 1200, 750 and 1500 Hz. | Debug log. Both builds write every log line to D: debug.log when drive D: is writable. Together with the c: aa test path, this looks like a development build. | . Vietnamese Log Strings . Most log strings are in Vietnamese with correct diacritics. This points to a Vietnamese-speaking author. It is a language observation, not attribution. . Text Translation . [+] Đang khử nhiễm: %ls | Disinfecting: %ls | . [+] Đã khôi phục OEP về 0x%X | Restored the OEP to 0x%X | . [✔] Khử nhiễm thành công. File co rút về đúng vách nguyên bản gốc (0x%X). | Disinfection successful. File shrunk back to its original boundary | . [+] File hoàn toàn sạch hoặc không chứa Metadata hợp lệ. Bỏ qua khâu dọn dẹp. | File is clean or has no valid metadata. Skipping cleanup. | . [!] InfectFile: Phát hiện phiên bản cũ hơn. Cho phép trượt luồng xuống Bước 2 để khử nhiễm... | Older version detected. Falling through to step 2 to disinfect... | . [ERROR] Khong tim thay 0xDEADBEEF trong s2mini_bin! | 0xDEADBEEF not found in s2mini_bin! | . Version 2 . We have one v2 core (0678e636, a memory dump) and no v2-infected host. The main changes from v1: . Two update hosts. It tries updatewinold[.]ddns[.]net first, then updatewin[.]ddns[.]net, both on TCP 8000. | No mutex. DllMain starts one thread, InitWorkerThread (v2 0xC404), which starts the five worker threads without a single-instance check. | New payload cipher. RC4 is replaced by a custom Feistel cipher on 8-byte blocks with 64 rounds per block. The key is the padded payload length, so the payload can be decrypted knowing only its size. The log says &quot;60000 rounds&quot;, which does not match the code. | New entry stub decoder. A position-keyed byte stream: k(i) = ror32(rol32((i ^ 0x9E3779B9) * 0x045D9F3B, 7) + i, 13). | New layer decryptor lookup. The layer decryptor searches for the 3-byte marker M^4 (4D 5E 34) and reads the key, multiplier and count after it. The layer cipher is unchanged. | New loader. It starts with the marker S2EN, resolves GetProcAddress by ROR-13 hash 0x7C0DFCAA, and contains an assembly copy of the Feistel decryptor. | . Decoding the Config . The script below decrypts the layer of an infected v1 host, parses the metadata box and decrypts the packed core DLL. It also handles the memory images, where the layer is already decrypted. It needs pefile, and upx for the second step. . import hashlib import re import struct import pefile # Layer decryptor head: call $+5 / pop ebp / lea edi,[ebp+3Ch] / mov ecx,[edi] / mov eax,[edi-8] / mov edx,[edi-4] S1_HEAD = bytes.fromhex(&#39;e8000000005d8d7d3c8b0f8b47f88b57fc&#39;) # Metadata box: last 0x28 bytes of the decrypted layer BOX = struct.Struct(&#39;&lt;8s8I&#39;) BOX_FIELDS = (&#39;rc4_key&#39;, &#39;stolen_size&#39;, &#39;oep_rva&#39;, &#39;stage1_rva&#39;, &#39;payload_off&#39;, &#39;layer_rva&#39;, &#39;payload_size&#39;, &#39;payload_rva&#39;, &#39;marker&#39;) def rol32(x, n): return ((x &lt;&lt; n) | (x &gt;&gt; (32 - n))) &amp; 0xFFFFFFFF def s1_decrypt(data, seed, mult): &quot;&quot;&quot;Layer decryptor cipher: a = rol32(a*mult + 0x13, 3); byte ^= a &amp; 0xFF; a += plaintext byte.&quot;&quot;&quot; out, a = bytearray(data), seed for i, c in enumerate(out): a = rol32((a * mult + 0x13) &amp; 0xFFFFFFFF, 3) out[i] = c ^ (a &amp; 0xFF) a = (a + out[i]) &amp; 0xFFFFFFFF return bytes(out) def rc4(key, data): S, j = list(range(256)), 0 for i in range(256): j = (j + S[i] + key[i % len(key)]) &amp; 0xFF S[i], S[j] = S[j], S[i] out, i, j = bytearray(), 0, 0 for c in data: i = (i + 1) &amp; 0xFF j = (j + S[i]) &amp; 0xFF S[i], S[j] = S[j], S[i] out.append(c ^ S[(S[i] + S[j]) &amp; 0xFF]) return bytes(out) def decode_host(data): &quot;&quot;&quot;Decrypt the layer of an infected v1 host and return (config, packed core DLL).&quot;&quot;&quot; pe = pefile.PE(data=data, fast_load=True) last = pe.sections[-1] sec = data[last.PointerToRawData:last.PointerToRawData + last.SizeOfRawData] head = sec.find(S1_HEAD) if head &lt; 0: return None, None kb = head + 5 + 0x3C # edi = (address of pop ebp) + 3Ch seed, mult, count = struct.unpack_from(&#39;&lt;III&#39;, sec, kb - 8) enc = sec[kb + 4:kb + 4 + count] # Infected files hold the layer encrypted. Process-memory images hold it already decrypted. for form, layer in ((&#39;infected file&#39;, s1_decrypt(enc, seed, mult)), (&#39;memory image&#39;, enc)): box = dict(zip(BOX_FIELDS, BOX.unpack_from(layer, len(layer) - BOX.size))) if box[&#39;marker&#39;] &gt;&gt; 24 != 0x20: continue core = rc4(box[&#39;rc4_key&#39;], layer[box[&#39;payload_off&#39;]:box[&#39;payload_off&#39;] + box[&#39;payload_size&#39;]]) if core[:2] != b&#39;MZ&#39;: continue config = { &#39;form&#39;: form, &#39;layer_seed&#39;: hex(seed), &#39;layer_mult&#39;: mult, &#39;rc4_key&#39;: box[&#39;rc4_key&#39;].hex(), &#39;oep_rva&#39;: hex(box[&#39;oep_rva&#39;]), &#39;stolen_size&#39;: box[&#39;stolen_size&#39;], &#39;marker&#39;: hex(box[&#39;marker&#39;]), &#39;core_sha256&#39;: hashlib.sha256(core).hexdigest(), } return config, core return None, None def core_strings(core): &quot;&quot;&quot;Pull the update hosts and version banner from an unpacked core DLL.&quot;&quot;&quot; return { &#39;update_hosts&#39;: sorted({m.decode() for m in re.findall(rb&#39;[a-z0-9.-]+ .ddns .net&#39;, core)}), &#39;version&#39;: [m.decode() for m in re.findall(rb&#39;VERSION ( d d/ d d/ d{4})&#39;, core)], &#39;request_name&#39;: [m.decode() for m in re.findall(rb&#39;Intel (R )[ -~]+? .exe&#39;, core)], } . Decrypt the layer of host a0c42a23 and print the metadata: . SAMPLE = &#39;a0c42a23fbcd3cd8c21db68517e18b5c229e9a2d95af62a5a57407ff92e48141&#39; with open(SAMPLE, &#39;rb&#39;) as f: config, core = decode_host(f.read()) for k, v in config.items(): print(f&#39;{k:12} {v}&#39;) . form infected file layer_seed 0x97b1a00 layer_mult 27 rc4_key 291c58e31f3b7a7d oep_rva 0x15bbd stolen_size 388 marker 0x20260715 core_sha256 ec55c4ba2ab0b530c4d613e9c0214611c734fc0d17e2fabec68ade33b980075c . The core is UPX packed, so the update host is not visible yet. Unpack it with upx -d and pull the strings: . import os import subprocess import tempfile with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as d: path = os.path.join(d, &#39;core.dll&#39;) with open(path, &#39;wb&#39;) as f: f.write(core) subprocess.run([&#39;upx&#39;, &#39;-q&#39;, &#39;-d&#39;, path], check=True, capture_output=True) with open(path, &#39;rb&#39;) as f: unpacked = f.read() info = core_strings(unpacked) info[&#39;update_hosts&#39;] = [h.replace(&#39;.&#39;, &#39;[.]&#39;) for h in info[&#39;update_hosts&#39;]] for k, v in info.items(): print(f&#39;{k:12} {v}&#39;) . update_hosts [&#39;updatewin[.]ddns[.]net&#39;] version [&#39;15/07/2026&#39;] request_name [&#39;Intel(R) HD Graphics Manager.exe&#39;] . The v2 core we have is a memory dump with its code in clear, so core_strings works on it directly: . with open(&#39;0678e636166df303548c8c2f4d75611304f7f01d5881ac6e4baad16dae0aa566&#39;, &#39;rb&#39;) as f: info = core_strings(f.read()) info[&#39;update_hosts&#39;] = [h.replace(&#39;.&#39;, &#39;[.]&#39;) for h in info[&#39;update_hosts&#39;]] for k, v in info.items(): print(f&#39;{k:12} {v}&#39;) . update_hosts [&#39;updatewin[.]ddns[.]net&#39;, &#39;updatewinold[.]ddns[.]net&#39;] version [&#39;23/08/2026&#39;] request_name [&#39;Intel(R) HD Graphics Manager.exe&#39;] . IOCs . Network . Indicator Notes . updatewin[.]ddns[.]net:8000 | v1 update host; v2 second choice | . updatewinold[.]ddns[.]net:8000 | v2 first choice | . Protocol strings sent by the client: DOWNLOAD, Intel(R) HD Graphics Manager.exe, READY. . Host . Artefact Value . Downloaded update | %TEMP% [a-z]{6}.exe | . Drive-root copy | X: [a-z]{4,6}.exe or .pif, read-only + hidden + system | . Autorun file | X: autorun.inf (old copy renamed autorun.inf.old if it cannot be deleted) | . Debug log | D: debug.log | . Mutex (v1) | @0826 | . Registry (read only) | HKCU Software Microsoft Windows CurrentVersion Run | . Infected File Traits . The entry section has characteristics 0xE0000020 (RWX). | The last section (.reloc or .rsrc) is enlarged and has characteristics 0xE2000020 or 0xE0000020. | DllCharacteristics has DYNAMIC_BASE and NX_COMPAT cleared. RELOCS_STRIPPED is set. | The checksum is 0 and the Security directory is zeroed, but the old certificate bytes remain as overlay. | The entry point is a junk stub. The v1 entry stub decoder 74 07 80 36 5F 46 49 EB F5 57 C3 is 0x100-0x160 bytes after it. | The v1 layer decryptor head E8 00 00 00 00 5D 8D 7D 3C 8B 0F 8B 47 F8 8B 57 FC 8D 77 04 89 F5 is in the last section. | Junk blocks start with 68 ?? F7 F4 FF 5?. | .",
